#---
# purpose: "a protocol call whose receiver type has no impl is a diagnostic naming the protocol, not a compiler crash"
# expect: diagnostic
# diagnostic.code: artifact/incomplete-semantic-plan
#---
#
# # protocol_without_impl
#
# `String.Chars` deliberately has no `@fallback_to_any` in Elixir: interpolating
# a map RAISES rather than printing something structural, and that refusal is
# the design's main safety property.
#
# fz could not express it. A protocol call with no matching impl reached an
# internal assertion -- "product materialization should have complete call edges
# after waits" -- and aborted the compiler with no span, no code, and no mention
# of the protocol or the receiver. The program did not fail; it failed to exist.
#
# The cause was an asymmetry rather than a missing check: in
# `materialize_call_edges`, a CLOSURE call whose callsite named no targets
# skipped quietly, while a DIRECT call in the same position forced the whole
# function to `None`, which the caller asserted could not happen. Protocol
# calls are direct calls.
#
# It was never specific to `String.Chars`: `Enum.count(:atom)` did the same
# thing on `Enumerable`, and had done all along.
#
# Elixir raises `Protocol.UndefinedError` at RUNTIME. fz answers at compile
# time, because the receiver's type is already known -- which is the better
# error, and is only available to a language that types the receiver.
def main() do
  dbg(to_string(%{a: 1}))
end
